  "long_biography": {
    "plain_text": "Flint joined the family-owned Flint Ink in 1964, when it generated about twelve million dollars in annual sales. He worked across the business and eventually succeeded his uncle as chairman and chief executive. That appointment made him the third generation to lead the company founded by his grandfather.\n\nUnder his stewardship, Flint Ink expanded beyond its American base into Europe and the Asia-Pacific region. It developed from a newspaper-ink specialist into a broad supplier of inks, colourants and related products. Annual revenue passed one billion dollars, and industry organisations honoured his technical and commercial contribution. The family agreed to sell the company to XSYS in 2005, the year Flint died, closing an era of direct family leadership.",
    "html": "<p>Flint joined the family-owned Flint Ink in 1964, when it generated about twelve million dollars in annual sales. He worked across the business and eventually succeeded his uncle as chairman and chief executive. That appointment made him the third generation to lead the company founded by his grandfather.</p>\r\n<p>Under his stewardship, Flint Ink expanded beyond its American base into Europe and the Asia-Pacific region. It developed from a newspaper-ink specialist into a broad supplier of inks, colourants and related products. Annual revenue passed one billion dollars, and industry organisations honoured his technical and commercial contribution. The family agreed to sell the company to XSYS in 2005, the year Flint died, closing an era of direct family leadership.</p>\r\n",
